' The body of scaling.puml / scaling-dark.puml. Not a diagram on its own: it
' carries no start marker, so PlantUML will not render this file directly.
'
' Careful: the start and end markers must not appear in this file at all, not
' even inside a comment. PlantUML scans for them lexically, so a comment naming
' one opens a second diagram here and the include fails with a line number
' pointing at neither file. See README.md.
'
' Split out because the README ships a light and a dark render of this diagram
' and the two must not drift. Every colour is a variable the wrapper supplies,
' so the dark render is dark throughout rather than a dark canvas behind white
' cards, which is worse than shipping no dark render at all.
'
' The post-render passes in README.md are not optional for either half.

' A post-render step widens this single font name into a fallback stack, so a
' viewer without Inter gets a sans-serif rather than the monospace a missing
' font can fall back to.
skinparam defaultFontName Inter

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 20
skinparam ranksep 46
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

title What scales horizontally, and what does not

header
<b>A shard key is a design decision, not a deployment one.</b>  Retrofitting one means changing every caller, so each service below
declares its unit now.  <b>Green scales out.  Amber scales out within a bound.  Red does not scale out at all</b>, and the red one is
red because of the wire protocol, not the implementation.
endheader

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "**scales out freely**: stateless over its own store" as FREE $c_green2 {
  component "**permissions**\nshard: **stateless**\n--\nACL evaluation walks the tree, and a busy\nchannel produces many identical in-flight\nqueries. **Coalesce them** rather than cache:\na cache needs invalidation, coalescing\ndoes not. «Discord's data-services trick»" as perms $c_card
  component "**userdata** · **text** · **pchat**\n**moderation** · **social** · **audit**\n**push** · **files/http** · **operator-api**\nshard: **stateless**\n--\nHistory-bearing services key rows by\n**UUIDv7**: coordination-free and\ntime-sortable. A central sequence is a\nbottleneck; UUIDv4 destroys index locality." as rest $c_card
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "**scales out, bounded by the key**" as BOUND $c_hub {
  component "**gateway**\nshard: **connection**\n--\nAny pod takes any connection. A client's\nTCP socket pins it for that connection's\nlifetime; **RESUME** lets it land elsewhere\nafter a restart." as gw $c_card
  component "**session-lifecycle**\nshard: **connection**\n--\nOne state machine per connection,\nindependent of every other." as slife $c_card
  component "**session-view**\nshard: **session id, from day one**\n--\nHolds a composed view per connected\nsession and updates all of them on every\nchange. Unsharded, that is one actor doing\nevery domain read,\n**Discord's guild-process bottleneck, relocated.**" as sview $c_amber
  component "**metadata** · **server-config**\nshard: **server instance**\n--\nOne actor per server instance, the Discord\nguild-process pattern. Within one server\ninstance it scales **vertically only**, which\nis also the answer to multiple server\ninstances in one deployment." as meta $c_card
  component "**screenshare**\nshard: **SFU session**\n--\nEach broadcast is independent, and Fancy\nclients learn their SFU endpoint from the\nanswer, so placement is free." as share $c_card
}

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "**must not scale out at all**" as SINGLE $c_indigo {
  component "**directory**\nshard: **none, one per server instance**" as dir $c_card
}

note bottom of SINGLE $c_indigo
  **Replicating this is not slower, it is wrong.** Each replica runs its own
  hourly timer, so N of them are N announcements of one server to the public
  list, and the list keys an entry by certificate fingerprint, which every
  replica shares. They would overwrite each other with racing user counts.
  It carries no request load to spread: one HTTPS POST an hour, and the counts
  in it come from session-view rather than from anything local. So there is no
  shard key to design, and the bound is **replicas: 1**.
end note

' ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
package "**does not scale out for legacy clients**" as PINNED $c_red {
  component "**voice**\nshard: **channel, never client**" as voice $c_card
}

note bottom of PINNED $c_red
  **Audio routing is per channel, so a channel's members must share a pod.**
  Client affinity (`sessionAffinity: ClientIP`) is actively wrong: it scatters
  a channel across pods, and there is **no inter-pod audio path** in this design.
  Discord assigns a voice *channel* to a voice server and **tells** the client
  which one. Mumble has no such field, and
  **a legacy client sends UDP to the port it made TCP to.**
  It cannot be redirected.
  Three ways out, and only the last scales:
  · **one voice pod per server instance**, vertical only
  · **a forwarding mesh between voice pods**, scales, costs a hop inside a 10 ms budget
  · **shard by channel and hand Fancy clients an explicit endpoint** in ServerSync,
    real scaling for Fancy, legacy pinned to the advertised pod
end note

note right of sview $c_amber
  **Why this one is amber and not green.**
  10 000 sessions means 10 000 composed views, all
  maintained by one actor. Sharding by session id
  makes it green; not choosing a key now makes it
  the first thing to fall over, and the hardest to
  fix, because every service in the domain calls it.
end note

note bottom of BOUND $c_card
  **Every actor mailbox is bounded and has a shed policy.**
  An unbounded inbox converts one slow consumer into an OOM, the
  specific failure Discord hit with Erlang process mailboxes under
  fanout. Applies to session-view per session and metadata per
  server instance alike.
end note

note bottom of FREE $c_card
  **Deadlines are not enough on their own.** A saturated service
  makes every caller wait its full deadline and *then* fail, burning
  capacity the whole time. A **circuit breaker** per service plus
  load shedding at the gateway fails fast instead.
end note

note as BUILT $c_neutral
  **Nothing here is sharded yet.** Every key above is a decision recorded
  before it becomes expensive, a shard key cannot be retrofitted, because
  changing one changes every caller. What runs today is one process per
  service, so the amber and red boxes are warnings about where growth stops,
  not descriptions of a running deployment.
end note

legend bottom
|= colour |= meaning |
|<$c_green2>   | scales out freely, add pods |
|<$c_hub>   | scales out along a declared key |
|<$c_amber>   | scales out **only if** the key is designed in now |
|<$c_red>   | cannot scale out; a protocol limit, not a code one |
|<$c_indigo>   | must not be replicated; correctness, not capacity |
endlegend
